#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "f12b3891-021d-5ae3-a767-f9d423408ce6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3567" :question/problem "Is the function $y=f(x)$, plotted below, continuous at $x=0$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/5a83932fdde3fa50945ddea33311a58d.png)" :question/worked-solution "Because there is an asymptote at $x=0$, it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nTherefore, the function $y=f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499855ad-f2de-5bd5-aa3a-e1e0312d1100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242124} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ee97ae-fba1-59ff-8520-8be5947cf413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242125} {:answer/id #uuid "974e1e94-75aa-51ef-ac38-60c44abfe821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242126} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8d8d83-d77a-5608-aa19-4cf4da602885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242127} {:answer/id #uuid "e4586339-52ea-5a00-b9b1-e687d62000a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c8d8d83-d77a-5608-aa19-4cf4da602885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242127} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd4a4d0a-1b4a-5c41-8cff-1321f5bd08c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242123}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d542cfea-06a6-5d32-836f-df32a05c8681" :question/math-academy-id "q-102115" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/d1d91b2d791d69c512a6bd2e7d523563de076a7f3afda53513234e799152ef8d.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=1$\n- $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ Since there is a jump at $x=-1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ Since there is a break at $x=1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"III only\".\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/57415045f7a1cc1c896756ff19cb17f90074151a25d12c56068fb6f86a064753.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99974464-05f0-5484-b5c8-5071961c2a35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/q-48039-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067248} {:answer/id #uuid "a2704f5a-4561-5560-84de-cc643e4